    {
      "id": 319,
      "type": "PixaromaNote",
      "pos": [
        1350.4695989660645,
        222.55671201638614
      ],
      "size": [
        750.18,
        665.0
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 6,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "Settings for your card",
      "properties": {
        "cnr_id": "ComfyUI-Pixaroma",
        "ver": "56e32d1676cab6700c54e4bdd37925f10867b970",
        "Node name for S&R": "PixaromaNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "{\"version\": 1, \"content\": \"<h1>SETTINGS FOR YOUR CARD</h1><p>\\n\\n</p><hr class=\\\"pix-note-hr-solid\\\" style=\\\"color: #b8b8b8\\\"><p>\\n\\n</p><p>This workflow only redraws a picture, so there is nothing to tune for quality except the render size, which has its own note next to that node.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"chip\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;What it costs</h2><table class=\\\"pix-note-grid\\\" style=\\\"--pix-note-grid-border: #f66744; --pix-note-grid-header-bg: #1a1a1a\\\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Measured on a 24 GB RTX 4090</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A render at about 1 megapixel</td><td>2.6 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>The biggest size, 1536 x 1536</td><td>4.5 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>The first run after starting ComfyUI</td><td>about 7 seconds longer</td></tr><tr><td>Memory in use at the busiest moment</td><td>21.4 GB</td></tr></tbody></table><p>\\n\\n</p><p>That 21.4 GB is what ComfyUI chose to use when it had 24 GB free, not what the workflow needs. It spreads out when there is room and packs down when there is not, so a smaller card can still run this. It was only measured on the big card here, so treat a small card as untested rather than ruled out.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"checkmark\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;The sampler</h2><p>Four steps at a CFG of 1 with <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">euler</span> and <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">simple</span>. Those are the turbo settings this model is built for and they do not need changing. If you want slightly crisper edges, try <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">6 to 8 steps</span> - it costs roughly that much more time.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"warning-v1\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;If you run out of memory</h2><ul><li><b>Lower the render size</b> first. It is by far the biggest lever here.</li><li>Close other programs using the graphics card, browsers included.</li><li>The 3D model itself barely matters. A 50 MB textured .glb loads no slower than a 6 MB one, because only the picture of it reaches the sampler.</li></ul><p>\\n\\n</p>\", \"buttonColor\": \"#f66744\", \"lineColor\": \"#f66744\", \"width\": 730, \"height\": 665, \"backgroundColor\": \"#2a2a2a\"}",
        ""
      ],
      "widgets_values_named": {
        "note_json": "{\"version\": 1, \"content\": \"<h1>SETTINGS FOR YOUR GRAPHICS CARD</h1><p>\\n\\n</p><hr class=\\\"pix-note-hr-solid\\\" style=\\\"color: #b8b8b8\\\"><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"chip\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;The four numbers that decide the speed</h2><p>\\n\\n</p><p>Everything else in the workflow can stay as it is. Each row says which node to look for.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><table class=\\\"pix-note-grid\\\" style=\\\"--pix-note-grid-border: #f66744; --pix-note-grid-header-bg: #1a1a1a\\\"><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Node on the canvas</th><th>Low VRAM</th><th>HQ</th><th>24 GB</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Upsample resolution</td><td>Upsample the shape</td><td>1024</td><td>1536</td><td>2048</td></tr><tr><td>Remesh resolution</td><td>Rebuild the mesh cleanly</td><td>512</td><td>768</td><td>1024</td></tr><tr><td>Triangles</td><td>Reduce to N triangles</td><td>200,000</td><td>700,000</td><td>1,500,000</td></tr><tr><td>Texture size</td><td>5. Texture size</td><td>2048</td><td>4096</td><td>4096</td></tr></tbody></table><p>\\n\\n</p><p>The <b>upsample resolution</b> is by far the biggest one, and how much it costs depends on your card. On the <b>6 GB RTX 2060</b>, dropping it from 1536 to 1024 took one test run from <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">20 minutes to 2 minutes</span>. On a <b>24 GB RTX 4090</b> the same change is much smaller: <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">39 seconds against 25</span>. Either way there is no difference you can see at normal viewing size, so lower it first if you are short of memory or time, and leave it high if you have the card for it.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><hr class=\\\"pix-note-hr-solid\\\" style=\\\"color: #b8b8b8\\\"><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"checkmark\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;Measured run times</h2><p>\\n\\n</p><p>Same picture, same seed, one object.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><table class=\\\"pix-note-grid\\\" style=\\\"--pix-note-grid-border: #f66744; --pix-note-grid-header-bg: #1a1a1a\\\"><thead><tr><th>Workflow</th><th>6 GB RTX 2060</th><th>24 GB RTX 4090</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Trellis 2 - Low VRAM</td><td>2 min 10 s</td><td>25 s</td></tr><tr><td>Trellis 2 - HQ + PBR Textures</td><td>40 min</td><td>2 min 28 s</td></tr><tr><td>Pixal3D - Low VRAM</td><td>6 min 20 s</td><td>37 s</td></tr><tr><td>Pixal3D - HQ + PBR Textures</td><td><span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">over 2 hours</span></td><td>3 min</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both columns were measured with Kitchen Attention enabled, using the same picture and seed. The first run after starting ComfyUI is a few seconds slower than the table while the models load into the card; run it again for the real speed.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><p>Pixal3D is slower on a small card because it also loads MoGe to work out the camera angle. Trellis 2 does not need it.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><p><span data-ic=\\\"warning-v1\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;<b>Pixal3D on HQ is not practical on a 6 GB card.</b> It made a much denser mesh than Trellis 2 (15.7 million faces against 11.8 million), and rebuilding a mesh that size runs on a single processor core, so the run was stopped after more than two hours. On a 6 GB card use <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">Pixal3D Low VRAM</span>, or use Trellis 2 for HQ. On a 24 GB card the same run finishes in 3 minutes.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><hr class=\\\"pix-note-hr-solid\\\" style=\\\"color: #b8b8b8\\\"><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"ai-brain\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;Why Free VRAM is in the workflow</h2><p>\\n\\n</p><p>Memory was sampled every 3 seconds through a full HQ run on the 6 GB test machine. It peaked at <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">5.47 GB of 6.00 GB</span>, so only 0.53 GB was spare at the worst moment.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><p>The <b>Free VRAM</b> node sits after the last step that needs the graphics card. In the trace it drops memory in use from 5.26 GB straight down to <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">1.25 GB</span>, right before the mesh and baking work, which is all done on the processor. On a big card it costs nothing.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><hr class=\\\"pix-note-hr-solid\\\" style=\\\"color: #b8b8b8\\\"><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"warning-v1\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;If you run out of memory</h2><p>\\n\\n</p><ul>\\n  <li>Use the <b>Low VRAM</b> workflow rather than the HQ one.</li>\\n  <li>Lower <b>Upsample resolution</b> first, then <b>Texture size</b>.</li>\\n  <li>Close other programs using the graphics card, browsers included.</li>\\n</ul><p>\\n\\n</p>\", \"buttonColor\": \"#f66744\", \"lineColor\": \"#f66744\", \"width\": 730, \"height\": 1010, \"backgroundColor\": \"#2a2a2a\"}",
        "note_dom": ""
      },
      "color": "#1d1d1d",
      "bgcolor": "#2a2a2a"
    },
