      "title": "About the render size",
      "properties": {
        "cnr_id": "comfyui-pixaroma",
        "Node name for S&R": "PixaromaNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "{\"version\": 1, \"content\": \"<h1>RENDER SIZE</h1><p>\\n\\n</p><hr class=\\\"pix-note-hr-solid\\\" style=\\\"color: #b8b8b8\\\"><p>\\n\\n</p><p>Every size in the list was run on a 24 GB RTX 4090 and checked. All seven came out at the exact size asked for, with no stretching and nothing repeated. <b>The three squares are listed first because they always keep the angle of your 3D view</b> - see the warning at the bottom.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><table class=\\\"pix-note-grid\\\" style=\\\"--pix-note-grid-border: #f66744; --pix-note-grid-header-bg: #1a1a1a\\\"><thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Shape</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1024 x 1024</td><td>square</td><td>2.6 s</td></tr><tr><td>1280 x 1280</td><td>square</td><td>3.5 s</td></tr><tr><td>1536 x 1536</td><td>square</td><td>4.5 s</td></tr><tr><td>1152 x 896</td><td>4:3</td><td>2.6 s</td></tr><tr><td>1216 x 832</td><td>3:2</td><td>2.6 s</td></tr><tr><td>1344 x 768</td><td>16:9</td><td>2.6 s</td></tr><tr><td>1536 x 640</td><td>wide</td><td>2.6 s</td></tr></tbody></table><p>\\n\\n</p><p>Anything around one megapixel costs the same 2.6 seconds, so pick the shape you want rather than the smallest one. Going bigger is what costs time. The first run after starting ComfyUI adds about 7 seconds while the model loads.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><p>Use <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">Portrait</span> / <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">Landscape</span> on the node to flip any of them, so a tall version of each is already there.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"warning-v1\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;Leave the 3D grid off</h2><p>The 3D view is photographed exactly as you see it, and that picture is what the model works from. With the grid on, its lines are captured too and can turn up in the finished image. It is off in this workflow. If you switch it on to line something up, switch it back before you Run.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"warning-v1\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;Tall shapes can tilt the object</h2><p>The 3D view is photographed at the size set on the <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">Load 3D</span> node, which is 1024 x 1024. The render size only changes the shape of the finished picture, not that photograph.</p><p>So if you pick a tall size, the model is given a square photograph and a tall frame to fill, and it is free to tilt the weapon to fill the space. Tested at 640 x 1536 on four seeds: <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">two kept the angle and two rotated it</span>, one of them by about sixty degrees.</p><p><b>Landscape and square are safe.</b> All seven sizes were clean in landscape. If you want a tall picture and the angle matters, set the <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">width and height on the Load 3D node</span> to the same shape as your render size, then drag inside the 3D view once so it takes a fresh photograph at that shape. Proven in testing: once the photograph matches the frame, both of the seeds that had rotated kept the angle.</p><p>Or use <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">6. Keep the Angle</span>, the other Flux Klein workflow in this set. It paints the picture on top of your 3D view instead of composing a new one, so the angle survives any shape, and the picture size comes from the Load 3D node rather than a list.</p><p>\\n\\n</p>\", \"buttonColor\": \"#f66744\", \"lineColor\": \"#f66744\", \"width\": 480.0, \"height\": 1165.0, \"backgroundColor\": \"#2a2a2a\"}"
      ],
      "color": "#14436c",
      "bgcolor": "#2a2a2a"
    }
  ],
